  "descriptionRussian": "Формы приказа и просьбы",
  "imageName": "exclamationmark.bubble",
  "intro": "The imperative mood is used for commands, requests, and instructions. Russian has two forms: informal (ты) and formal/plural (вы). Formation depends on the present tense stem: if it ends in a vowel, add -й/-йте; if it ends in a consonant, add -и/-ите (or -ь/-ьте if stem-stressed). Reflexive verbs (-ся) keep their particle: after a consonant or -й it stays -ся, after a vowel it becomes -сь (садись!/садитесь! = sit down). Negative commands often use the imperfective: Не говори! (Don't speak!) Some verbs change their stem or are irregular and must be memorized.",
  "rules": [
    "To form the imperative, start from the они present tense form, remove -т, and add the appropriate suffix.",
    "Reflexive verbs add the particle after the imperative ending: -ся after a consonant or -й, -сь after a vowel. Садись!/Садитесь! (sit down), Умывайся!/Умывайтесь! (wash up), Не волнуйся! (Don't worry!)",
    "Some verbs change their stem: мыть → мой/мойте (wash), сесть → сядь/сядьте (sit down), пить → пей/пейте (drink). Note садись (imperfective: settle in) vs сядь (perfective: sit down now).",
    "Negative commands typically use imperfective (Не говори! = Don't speak!), while positive commands can use either aspect.",
    "Adding пожалуйста after the imperative makes any command a polite request: Скажите, пожалуйста! (Please tell me!)",
    "Some common imperatives are irregular and must be memorized: дай/дайте (give), ешь/ешьте (eat), пей/пейте (drink)."
  ],
  "examples": [
    "Читай книгу! (Read the book!), informal",
    "Читайте, пожалуйста! (Please read!), formal/plural",
    "Говори громче! (Speak louder!), informal",
    "Не забудьте! (Don't forget!, formal/plural), perfective"
  ],
